Believe me, I feel for you. In my current game, I managed to blow up
my BoH on Medusa's level, despite knowing exactly what causes it.
Sometimes, you just get careless. I hadn't made *that* mistake in over
two years, since before my first (and only) ascension. Luckily, all of
my really critical gear was in general inventory at the time, so I
mostly just lost things that would have been really nice to have, but
weren't essential. The bad news is that whatever is in a BoH when it
explodes is well and truly gone forever.

The good news is that, all things considered, this is a recoverable
error. You cannot put the bell, candelabrum, or book of the dead into
a container, so it isn't possible to render your game unwinnable this
way. This is probably also why most (all?) quest artifacts have to be
carried in general inventory to get their magical effects.

> What else do I need before I return?

Two-weaponing Grayswandir and a silver saber is a very lethal
combination (for the denizens of Gehennom, not you), so you're in good
shape there. If you had Mjollnir and lost it in the bag, I wouldn't
sweat it. In most respects, Grayswandir is a better weapon anyway,
particularly in Gehennom. Since you can't two-weapon with two
artifacts, you aren't really missing anything. If you can, enchant
them both to +7, but be aware that you can destroy it if you try to
enchant it up from +6. You'll need to find a way to lower it to +5 if
you want to enchant it safely. If you only have one or two scrolls of
enchant weapon, I'd go for the silver saber first. When two-weaponing,
either both weapons hit, or neither of them do. A +6 Grayswandir/+5
silver saber combo is just as effective as +7 Grayswandir/+3 silver
saber, and (probably) takes only one scroll instead of two.

You didn't mention a source of magic resistance. I would hazard a
guess that with -18AC after losing protection (and from the fact that
you made to gehennom in the first place) that you have GDSM.
Otherwise, MR is absolutely your #1 priority. There are nasties in
Gehennom that use, for example, a touch of death attack, from which
reflection alone will not protect you. The cramped confines of
Gehennom do not make taking these nasties out at a distance a reliable
strategy. Again, I'm not sure how much you want to be spoiled, but
there are several ways of doing this. Gray Dragons may occasionally
show up in the deeper levels, but hanging out in or around Gehennom is
not advisable without MR.

Ideally, you should try to buy back your protection. Contrary to some
peoples' beliefs you really don't need to have 600+HP and <=-40 AC
just enter Gehennom. My first ascension was a Valk with 484HP and
-35AC, and I felt like the planes were pretty easy. If this is a first
ascension and you don't want to leave anything to chance, I'd aim for
~300-350HP and -30AC before waking the Wizard. You can handle most or
all of Gehennom with less than that, but remember that once you've
killed the Wizard of Yendor, you will not get any relief until the
Astral Plane. There really won't be time to mess around with
enchanting armor and weapons or buying protection once you've crossed
that line.

If you need more gold for the protection racket, there are several
ways to get it. You can mine every level for gold, using some potions
of object detection or scrolls of gold detection. Clear out every
vault, leprechaun hall, and treasure zoo. If Ft. Ludios exists in your
game, raid it. If that's still not enough, you could take more extreme
measures. You can use a pet in minetown either to do the credit
cloning scam or to simply kill the shopkeepers and take their gold.
Be aware that many people on r.g.r.n frown on killing Izchak. Some
also dislike stealing from him. If another priest exists in your game,
you can even let a strong pet kill him, get all your protection money
back, and spend it at the other priest. There is always a priest of
Moloch in the Valley of the Dead temple. Yes, he accepts donations for
protection. No, it doesn't make any sense.

You also need a unicorn horn -- at least uncursed, preferably blessed
-- if you don't have one on hand. These are pretty easy to obtain.

> What happened to my bag? And what do I need to look out for
> now that I'm venturing into new territory?

If you aren't opposed to some spoilage, recommended reading includes
"I finally arrived in Gehennom. Now what?" and "Maniac's Ascension
Guide". These will give you a good idea of what you should be doing to
prepare for the end game.

For the most part, Gehennom is just "more of the same" after you've
seen the first level. In fact, it's a common complaint that Gehennom
is too long in vanilla NetHack, or at least needs more variation
instead of level after level after level of random mazes. The deeper
you go, the more difficult the random monsters become. Generally, the
nastiest monsters outside of the planes will already begin showing up
in the upper levels of Gehennom. So really, this just means that
nasties like liches, titans, and mind flayers will begin showing up
more frequently. Genociding a few select species will make your life
easier if you don't mind breaking the genocideless conduct.

